module bindings.python.pillow_unrestricted_format_decode;

// Pillow documents Image.open's `formats` argument as "a list or tuple of
// formats to attempt to load the file in. This can be used to restrict the set
// of formats checked", and says that passing None -- the default, and what a
// call with no `formats` keyword at all does -- tries all supported formats.
// The plugin that runs is therefore selected by the file's own bytes rather
// than by the caller, over the whole registered plugin set. One of those
// plugins is EPS, whose reading Pillow documents as offloading to an external
// Ghostscript process whenever Ghostscript is installed, so an upload that
// sniffs as PostScript reaches a program that executes it. An extension
// whitelist on the uploaded name decides nothing here: the decoder never
// consults the name.
//
// The one restriction the caller can impose is the `formats` keyword, and it is
// read per call site, so a restricted open and an unrestricted one in the same
// function are told apart and a safe spelling in one method does not silence an
// unsafe one elsewhere. Both `Image.open` and `PIL.Image.open` are matched
// because both are Pillow's own documented import forms. The label is the
// upload sink the unrestricted-upload rule already judges, so a decode of
// genuinely local artifacts -- an asset bundled with the code, a file the same
// process just wrote -- reports nothing: it is the taint requirement that
// decides, not this binding. Residual, in the false-negative direction only: a
// string literal argument of the same call containing the substring "formats"
// satisfies the negation, and Pillow offers no spelling of the restriction
// other than the keyword.
binding pythonPillowOpenWithoutFormatRestriction {
  query pattern callExpr where callee.path in ["Image.open", "PIL.Image.open"]
    and not (args.any.literal contains "formats")
  emit sink code.UnsafeUpload at args[0]
}
